\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A translation need not commute with a local gauge}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Work over #{\mathbb Q} or an odd prime field and
choose #{a\ne0} in #{X}. Let the Hurwitz vertex
gauge be #{g(0)=-1} and #{g(x)=1} elsewhere.
For #{f=\delta_0\otimes1\otimes1}, the values
at #{-a} are
##{(\tau_a\mathcal G_g f)(-a)=-1,\qquad
       (\mathcal G_g\tau_a f)(-a)=1.}
They differ since #{2\ne0}. Hence the generated
translation algebra in \ref{fdip-00W3} is not
a commutant of arbitrary local gauges.
Likewise its translations are fixed-background
operator symmetries only under the criterion
of \ref{fdip-00W2}. The scalar geometric
restriction in \ref{fdip-00VN} concerns the
declared constant right actions, not every
operation in the larger generated algebra.}
